<h2>The dispute: a 1991 subcontract, a 1992 termination</h2>
<p>NBCC engaged MS Sharma Enterprises in 1991 for flooring and cladding work at Vashi Railway Station. In September 1992 NBCC terminated the contract and encashed the contractor's bank guarantees. The arbitrator found the termination wrongful and awarded the contractor several sums, together with interest at 10% from 22 September 1992 — covering the pre-reference period, the pendente lite period while the arbitration ran, and the post-award period.</p>
<p>The interest component ran into trouble in court. A learned Single Judge set aside the pre-reference and pendente lite interest, and the Division Bench dismissed the contractor's appeal. The contractor carried the question to the Supreme Court: could Clause 26 of the Special Conditions of Contract really strip the arbitrator of the power to award interest for the period before the award?</p>

<h2>Clause 26 and the statutory default</h2>
<p>Clause 26 provided that no claim for interest would be entertained by the corporation in respect of balance payments or deposits held up due to disputes, delays in monthly or final payments, <em>"or otherwise"</em>. The statutory backdrop is Section 31(7) of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996:</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Provision</th><th>What it says</th><th>Can parties contract out?</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 31(7)(a)</td><td>The tribunal may award interest on the whole or part of the money awarded, for the whole or part of the period between the cause of action and the date of the award.</td><td>Yes — the provision applies "unless otherwise agreed by the parties".</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 31(7)(b)</td><td>The sum directed to be paid carries post-award interest until payment, unless the award directs otherwise.</td><td>No — the parties' contract cannot exclude it; only the award itself can modulate it.</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The contractor's argument was essentially one of construction: Clause 26, read narrowly, dealt only with interest on delayed running payments and deposits, not with interest on damages for wrongful termination awarded in arbitration. On the ejusdem generis approach, "or otherwise" should take colour from the specific situations preceding it.</p>

<h2>What the Supreme Court held</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>"Or otherwise" extends, it does not limit</h4><p>The Court held that the phrase is residuary and cannot be cut down to situations resembling delayed payments. It operates as a bar on interest for amounts withheld for any reason — including sums caught up in arbitral disputes.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>The arbitrator exceeded his jurisdiction</h4><p>Because Section 31(7)(a) yields to the parties' agreement, the award of pre-reference and pendente lite interest travelled beyond the arbitrator's mandate. The courts below were right to sever it.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Post-award interest survives</h4><p>Section 31(7)(b) is not subject to contractual exclusion. The post-award interest in the contractor's favour was maintained, as were the principal sums awarded.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The Court anchored its reasoning in its earlier line of authority on contractual interest bars, and dismissed the appeal, finding no error in the Division Bench's judgment.</p>

<h2>Why the decision matters in commercial practice</h2>
<p>Interest is often the largest single head in old construction and supply claims — in this very case the principal dispute dated to 1992, so three decades of interest dwarfed the principal. The judgment confirms that the battle over interest is frequently won or lost at the drafting stage, years before any dispute arises.</p>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For contractors and suppliers:</strong> read the interest clause before signing. A bar framed with sweeping words such as "or otherwise" will likely be enforced literally, leaving only post-award interest.</li>
    <li><strong>For employers and buyers:</strong> a well-drafted exclusion clause remains one of the most effective limits on arbitral exposure, since it binds the tribunal itself.</li>
    <li><strong>For award-holders:</strong> where the contract contains such a clause, build the claim on heads the clause does not touch, and do not treat pendente lite interest as automatic.</li>
    <li><strong>For challenges under Section 34:</strong> an award of interest contrary to a contractual bar is a recognised ground of interference, because it goes to the tribunal's jurisdiction rather than the merits.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2>The position after the judgment</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><h4>Contract silent on interest</h4><p>The arbitrator retains full discretion under Section 31(7)(a) to award pre-reference and pendente lite interest at a reasonable rate.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><h4>Contract bars interest "or otherwise"</h4><p>No pre-award interest can be granted at all. Post-award interest under Section 31(7)(b) remains payable from the date of the award until realisation.</p></div>
</div>
